GAL16V8
bbproto

CLK  MATCH A0    A1    A2    A3    RO    RI    RESET GND
OE   INHREQ WR3  WR2   WR1   WR0   RD2   RD1   RD0   VCC


;
; Starting point for a breadboard prototype's glue logic, in an ATF16V8C-7PU on the
; breadboard, behind the breadboard adapter. Compile with galette. Keep each equation on
; one line; galette rejects a continuation line that starts with "+".
;
; Inputs, all from the adapter's ribbon cable:
;   CLK     8 MHz clock (pin 1 is the register clock in registered mode)
;   MATCH   active low: the CPU is accessing the adapter's 16-byte window
;   A0..A3  register select within the window
;   RO      active low: the CPU is reading (RAM output strobe)
;   RI      active low: the CPU is writing (RAM input strobe)
;   RESET   active low: the Minimal's reset line, for clearing prototype state
;           (no equation uses it yet)
;
; Pin 11 (OE) is the output enable for the registered outputs in registered mode. Tie it
; to GND on the breadboard. A prototype with no registered outputs gets pins 1 and 11 back
; as ordinary inputs.
;
; Example register map. The adapter's SW1 sets the window's base nibble, shown here as
; "x" (SW1 = $C makes the window $FEC0..$FECF):
;
;   Address | CPU writes (WRn)       | CPU reads (RDn)
;   --------+------------------------+------------------------
;   $FEx0   | WR0, e.g. latch byte 0 | RD0, e.g. read byte 0
;   $FEx1   | WR1, e.g. latch byte 1 | RD1, e.g. read byte 1
;   $FEx2   | WR2, e.g. command      | RD2, e.g. status
;   $FEx3   | WR3, e.g. reset/clear  | (nothing, reads RAM)
;   $FEx4.. | (nothing)              | (nothing, reads RAM)
;   $FExF   |                        |
;
; Timing budget (see the adapter README, "Building a prototype's logic"): from the moment
; MATCH, A0..A3, and RO arrive on the breadboard, INH_REQ must be out within about 14 ns
; worst case and read data must be on the cable within about 27 ns. One pass through a -7
; part takes 7.5 ns, so INHREQ below is decoded straight from the input pins rather than
; from the RD outputs: feeding outputs back through the array would be a second pass.
;


;
; Write strobes: registered, active high, exactly one CLK cycle long.
;
; The CPU's RI goes low about 50 ns into the write cycle and stays low until about 50 ns
; into the next one, so the strobe is sampled on the CLK edge that ends the write cycle.
; The CPU's data is still on the bus for roughly 25 ns after that strobe rises, so clock
; each data latch (74AHCT374 / 574) on the strobe's RISING edge. The falling edge comes a
; cycle later, after the data is gone.
;

; CPU write to $FEx0
WR0.R = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A1 * /A0 * /RI * RO

; CPU write to $FEx1
WR1.R = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A1 * A0 * /RI * RO

; CPU write to $FEx2
WR2.R = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * A1 * /A0 * /RI * RO

; CPU write to $FEx3
WR3.R = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * A1 * A0 * /RI * RO


;
; Read enables: combinational, active low. Each one drives the output enable of the
; buffer or register that puts that byte on the breadboard's data bus. Use 74AHCT parts
; (or put a 74AHCT245/541 in front of a slower source): the budget leaves about 20 ns
; for the output enable after this PLD's 7.5 ns.
;

; CPU read from $FEx0
/RD0 = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A1 * /A0 * /RO * RI

; CPU read from $FEx1
/RD1 = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A1 * A0 * /RO * RI

; CPU read from $FEx2
/RD2 = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * A1 * /A0 * /RO * RI


;
; INH request to the adapter: active high on every CPU read the prototype answers, that
; is $FEx0, $FEx1, or $FEx2. The first term covers $FEx0 and $FEx1 (A1 low), the second
; covers $FEx0 and $FEx2 (A0 low). It repeats the read decode instead of ORing RD0..RD2
; so it stays a single pass through the PLD.
;

INHREQ = /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A1 * /RO * RI + /MATCH * /A3 * /A2 * /A0 * /RO * RI

DESCRIPTION

Template glue logic for a Minimal 64x4 card prototyped on a breadboard behind the
breadboard adapter: four registered write strobes, three read enables, and INH request.
